      "writtenOn": "2026-08-17T20:33:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With modern C++, the generally preferred method to handling object references is to use \"smart pointers\" (e.g. `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) and construct those using `std::make_unique`/`std::make_shared`. This ensures that the objects persist in memory outside of the class and are automatically destroyed via the default destructor when they go out of scope. There are plenty of examples of how to handle that in the fan and power repos, so I would encourage you to take a look at those.\n\nAs an example here, these would probably be something like\n\n```\nstd::vector\u003cstd::unique_ptr\u003csdbusplus::match\u003e\u003e _propMatches;\nstd::vector\u003cstd::unique_ptr\u003csdbusplus::match\u003e\u003e _ifaceMatches;\n```",
      "revId": "318ec6947271d627bbf15de86c23abb8b3c7d4c6",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
